WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
 So welcome back.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.640
 Let's talk a little bit more about some more complex

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.680
 conditional statements that sort of build on this concept

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.680
 of if and if-then-else.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.400
 The first thing we're going to look at is the multi-way branch.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.280
 And so the idea is it's kind of like the if-then-else where

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.020
 you're going to pick one of two, but now we

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.540
 can pick one of three, or one of four, or one of five.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.240
 And it introduces a new concept called the LF.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.160
 The LF is another reserved word inside Python.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.800
 And the way it works is it's probably best to look at this

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.000
 here where it checks the first one, and if it's a true,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.240
 then it runs that, and then it's done.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.400
 It doesn't check them all.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.040
 It's not like it sees that there are two logical conditions.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.760
 It actually checks them, the first one,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.080
 and how you order these matters, as we'll see in a bit.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.560
 And so if the first one is true, it runs.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.040
 If the first one is false and the second one is true,

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.880
 it runs this one, and it's done.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.840
 And if neither of them are true, it falls through,

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.560
 and there's an else clause that is otherwise, and it runs that.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.960
 So basically, it's going to run one and then skip the other two,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.480
 or it is going to skip one, skip two, and then run this one.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.100
 But it only runs, in this case, one of them.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.520
 But the important thing is it checks these questions in order.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.440
 And it doesn't check the second question

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.040
 until it finds that the first-- it doesn't

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.360
 check the second question until it

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.720
 knows the first question is false.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.720
 So if the first question is true, you're done.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.320
 You're done, and you're done with this.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.800
 You're done with the whole block at that point.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.520
 So only one of these three is going to execute in that block.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.560
 So here's sort of some examples of this.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.640
 If we, for example, have x equals 0,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.800
 it's going to come down here.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.760
 x is less than true.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.760
 That's true.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.240
 So it runs this code, and then it skips, skips, skips,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.240
 down to that.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.400
 And so it's like this, runs that code,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.200
 and then skips to the end.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.680
 On the other hand, if it's 5, then this is false,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.880
 and it skips that, and it checks this, this is true.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.800
 It runs this code, and then it's done, skips to the end.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.320
 Goes like false, true, run, end.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.680
 And then if x is like 20, for example, it runs.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.400
 It runs, false, false, run the else clause, and you're done.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.840
 So skip, skip, oh, else, run that code, and you're done.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.120
 So in this case, we ran that, and we didn't run that,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.520
 and we didn't run that.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.880
 Again, one of them is going to run.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.120
 They're checked in order.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.720
 These questions are checked in order, not out of order.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.720
 It doesn't look ahead.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.440
 It just checks in the order that you wrote it.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.600
 You're the one that wrote that order.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.880
 And so there's a couple of variations on this multi-way.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.240
 You can have no else.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.400
 You can have no else, as in this case.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.040
 And this just means that it might not run any of them.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.440
 In this case, x is 5, so it's not less than 2,

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.000
 but then it runs this one.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.520
 But if x was like 50, for example, if x was 50,

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.840
 then this would be false, and it would skip,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.960
 and this would still be false, and it would skip,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.240
 and either of these two would run.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.200
 So if you don't have an else, you're

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.160
 not guaranteed that one of them is going to run,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.880
 because else is like the catch-all.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.160
 If the other ones are all false, then the else

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.200
 is the one that runs.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.040
 Similarly, you can have many LFs.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.440
 But this is where it's really important for you

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.960
 to make sure you know what order they're being taken in.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.560
 So if this is true, it runs.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.800
 It goes all the way to the bottom.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.480
 If it's false, false, false, true, it runs this one,

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.880
 and it's done.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.360
 If, on the other hand, it looks at it as false-- oops,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.960
 go back, go back-- if it runs false, false, false, false,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.440
 they're all false, then it runs the else.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.800
 This one has an else, this one didn't have an else.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.880
 They don't have to have them.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.160
 The key is you can have more than one of these LS.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.800
 So I got a couple little things.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.680
 I'll let you pause right now and look at-- the question is,

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.840
 are there, looking at the three lines or four lines of code,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.840
 x equals something.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.640
 Are there lines of code that will never execute,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.520
 regardless of the value for x?

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.640
 And I'll let you pause and think about it,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.100
 and then I'll explain it to you.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.800
 OK, hopefully you paused and thought about it

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:38.920
 as long as you like.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.400
 So let me now explain it to you.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.320
 So we come in here, and if x is less than or equal to 2,

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.720
 it's going to run this first thing.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.240
 And if x is greater than or equal to 2,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.280
 it's going to run this.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.720
 And if neither of those are true,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.840
 then it's going to run this.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.040
 Well, the weird thing is, all numbers

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.480
 are either less than 2 or greater than or equal to 2.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.720
 I carefully constructed this to the point

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.360
 where it would never run this line of code.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.400
 It is either going to run this one or run that one,

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.040
 but it's not going to ever run this one.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.240
 So that was kind of like a weird dysfunctional one

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.480
 that I constructed.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.840
 This other one is a little different.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.880
 If x is less than 2, we do this.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.840
 If x is less than 20, we do that.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.440
 If x is less than 10, we do that.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.200
 And if none of those are true, we do that.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.000
 Well, the problem here is between these two lines.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.880
 The problem is, if something's less than 10,

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.260
 like 6, for example, it's also less than 20.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.800
 So even though there might be values for which this is true,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.480
 those also are going to have this true.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.600
 So for something like 6, it's going to run here,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.660
 and it's not even going to look at this.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.400
 That's the point.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.840
 It doesn't even look at this.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.800
 And so that's-- I mean, I could have made this more sensible

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.280
 if I had moved this little block of code up to there.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.840
 So this is where the order in which you

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.000
 choose your questions, the way you put these LFs together,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.400
 matters because it doesn't look at all of them.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.320
 It only looks as long as it can.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.480
 As long as it sees falses, then it

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.760
 keeps on going to the next one.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.800
 But as soon as it doesn't see a false, it doesn't continue.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.160
 So the last conditional structure

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.800
 we'll talk about is the try and accept structure.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.920
 If you know any other languages like C++ or Java or JavaScript,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.880
 you're like, whoa, that's kind of an advanced concept.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.720
 But it turns out in Python, because of Python's propensity

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.520
 to throw tracebacks in situations where you kind of

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.080
 would like to recover, it turns out

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.000
 you kind of have to use it a little more and a little earlier

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.360
 in your programming skill.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.280
 So the problem is what if there is a line of code

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.720
 and you absolutely know it's going to make a traceback?

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.840
 It's going to blow up.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.080
 But you don't want to blow up.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.840
 I don't want to have code blow up.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.300
 If you're using my autograder and you

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.600
 see a traceback in my autograder,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.720
 that's kind of like I consider that a failure.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.640
 I could put an error like, hey, you entered blank data

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.360
 or you didn't enter a number.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.680
 But a traceback, that just seems like I'm

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:57.760
 too lazy as a programmer.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.560
 So we as programmers are supposed

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.280
 to anticipate parts of our code that

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.780
 are going to blow up potentially based on perhaps the user's

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.960
 input and then do something about it.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.840
 And that's what the try and accept are for.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.800
 If you take this little dangerous piece of code

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.000
 that might break and might blow up,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.560
 and you surround it with a try and it says,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.760
 this might blow up.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.280
 And if it fails, run this code down here.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.480
 So that's the try.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.340
 And if you get an exception, the accept

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:26.600
 is kind of like if you get an exception.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:30.480
 And the problem is if you are running code-- here's

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.800
 a little bit of code-- we put hello Bob in,

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:34.240
 and we convert it to an integer.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.200
 And we know from past experience that this blows up.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.400
 You can't take hello Bob and convert it to an integer.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.720
 It's just going to blow up.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.040
 The problem is-- and here we are.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.560
 It says, oh, you blew up on line two.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:47.460
 That's great.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.600
 And I'm not very happy with hello Bob and whatever.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.880
 But the important thing is your program stops.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:57.840
 These other lines, they don't-- oops.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.720
 These other lines, they don't exist.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.960
 It doesn't go any further.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.920
 Remember, the traceback is Python is really confused.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.840
 And I don't know what to do next.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.680
 So Python is just going to be conservative and stop.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.120
 So Python stops, and your program stops.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.400
 No matter how much error checking you put down here,

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:17.080
 it doesn't matter because it's gone.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.280
 It's all gone.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.560
 And like I said, we take this kind of personally

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.080
 because the code that you write is like the you being put

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.380
 into the computer, giving it instructions.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.840
 And if the code blows up, well, that sort of wipes you out.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.320
 You're not in the game anymore.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.240
 You're not able to do anything.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.520
 So we want to be able to-- especially in these situations

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.000
 where we can anticipate that an error that

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.260
 might happen in the normal course or your program's

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:46.140
 execution might be something that you want to compensate for.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.200
 And that's what the try and accept does.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.360
 So here's a bit of code for the try and accept.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.160
 And we just have two little bits of straight line code.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.240
 And so we put a string in here that's hello Bob.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.640
 And then we're going to convert it to an integer.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.280
 This is the dangerous code.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.040
 This code, in this case, with hello Bob,

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.000
 is going to do a traceback.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.040
 And so we say try, and then we indent the dangerous code.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.320
 And then we add this little accept bit.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.160
 If it works, the accept is ignored.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.160
 If this blows up, it runs the accept.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.680
 So in this code, it's going to come in.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.040
 It's going to try this.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.280
 This is going to blow up.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.820
 But instead of giving a traceback,

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.900
 it's going to say, oh, I've got an available accept.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.760
 I'm going to run this accept code,

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.200
 and then I'm going to continue on.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.120
 And so that prints out first negative 1.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.680
 So because we set this variable, Ister, to negative 1,

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.080
 like a little flag telling us that something went wrong.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.400
 And then we keep on going.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.080
 And now we have put in 1, 2, 3.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.240
 The digit's 123.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.560
 The digit's 123.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:41.640
 And now it's going to work.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.240
 But we still have it in a try block.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.560
 And then this one works.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.240
 It does not blow up.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.680
 And then it ignores the accept block.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.920
 So the accept block is only triggered

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.980
 when something goes wrong in the code.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.320
 It is ignored if something doesn't go wrong.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.200
 So it's like you bought an insurance policy

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:57.560
 on this line of code.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:00.360
 And when things go wrong, your accept block

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.800
 springs into action and does whatever

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.200
 it is that you want it to do in the case of an error.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.320
 So that's a pretty useful thing.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.800
 You've got to be a little bit careful that you don't overuse

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:11.320
 it.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:15.000
 Because if you put more than one line inside the try part,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.520
 and one of the lines blows up, it

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.400
 doesn't come back to the try block.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.020
 And so in this one here, we have kind of a simple, silly one

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:25.680
 where we set the string, we're worried about some stuff.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.200
 Well, the print statement's never going to blow up.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.960
 So it's a bad idea to put it in try except anyways.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:31.200
 Then we do this conversion.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.200
 And that's the dangerous part.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.280
 And in this one, it's going to blow up.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.840
 And so then it's going to go to the accept block,

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.000
 and then run the accept block, and then continue.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.560
 What it does not do, what it doesn't do,

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:45.920
 is somehow go back and finish this.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:47.920
 So these lines are gone.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.880
 So if you look at it like this, this works.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.400
 The try starts.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:52.800
 Hello.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.040
 This blows up.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:55.160
 It goes to the accept.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.280
 It runs the accept, and it continues on.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:00.000
 Never runs that code.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.360
 So it's not like you took out an insurance on the whole block.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.360
 Any of those lines can blow up in the block.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:07.840
 But whichever line blows up, that

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:12.680
 is the last line that's executing in that block.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.920
 So you tend to want-- in this particular example,

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.520
 you would probably-- the print statement would go out there,

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.320
 and this print statement would come down here.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.920
 And you would only put in your try block a single line of code

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.360
 that you think might blow up, because you kind of know print

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:28.400
 statements aren't going to blow up.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.800
 So this is an example that's a more common real world example,

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.840
 where the user is going to type some data.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.940
 And that's users that get us in trouble.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.240
 So our program starts by asking the user enter a number.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.400
 And we know that this could be dangerous.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:47.760
 So we're going to put the conversion from string

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.560
 to integer in a try block.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.400
 And we're going to set negative 1 if that's a failure.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.880
 And then if it's greater than 0, we'll say, nice work.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.960
 And if it's less than 0, well, not a number.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.000
 So first time we run this program,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.040
 out comes enter a number.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.680
 We type in 42, which is a string.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.280
 That 42 goes back into roster, runs in here.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:08.120
 This runs.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:08.920
 It's fine.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.280
 That becomes a 42 number.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.120
 So we skip the accept block.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.360
 And Ival is greater than 0.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:17.000
 We print out nice work, and we skip the else.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.600
 So it says, nice work.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.360
 On the other hand, if we run it again this time,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:26.000
 the input says enter a number.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:27.080
 And we're silly.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:31.800
 We enter the word 42, but in words, 40, F-O-U-R-T-Y.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.040
 So that's a string, and that goes into roster.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.400
 And then the execution continues.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:36.640
 We run in here.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.440
 And now this is going to blow up.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:40.400
 That's going to blow up.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:43.120
 Normally, we would see a traceback right there.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:44.320
 There would be a traceback.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:46.600
 But we're not going to, because we put this calculation

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:47.780
 in a try and accept block.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.640
 It's going to immediately run the accept block,

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:52.760
 set Ival to negative 1, continue on with the program.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.320
 See, you are not blown up at this point.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.400
 And if Ival is greater than 0, well, it's negative 1.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:58.840
 So we're going to hit the else clause

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:00.520
 and print out not a number.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.800
 So we've done error detection.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.760
 The user set something that caused a line of our code

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:06.920
 to kind of blow up.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.200
 But we put that line in a try and accept block,

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.200
 and so we caught it.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:14.320
 And so we dealt with that fact.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.320
 So in summary in this, we talked about if statements.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.520
 We talked about else.

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:22.360
 We talked about try and accept, how important indentation

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:26.320
 is to mark blocks where they begin in the end,

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:28.560
 and an else if, and try except.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:34.020
 So up next, we're going to talk about loops and iteration.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:51.560
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

